(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which history precedes viral myocarditis?","choices":["A flulike illness 1 to 2 weeks before","Chest trauma 1 to 2 days before","Years of heavy alcohol intake","An emotional shock hours before"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viral myocarditis follows a recent flulike syndrome 1 to 2 weeks earlier, with fever, myalgia, fatigue, respiratory symptoms or gastroenteritis. Only then do heart failure symptoms appear, such as exercise intolerance, shortness of breath and chest pain, in a person with no underlying cardiac dysfunction. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Why are nitrates avoided in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["They cause complete heart block","They raise the blood pressure","They thicken the septum further","They worsen outflow obstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates reduce preload and afterload, and drugs that do so are avoided, if possible, in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy because they may increase symptoms by aggravating outflow tract obstruction. The initial drug, a beta blocker, works the other way, enhancing diastolic filling and decreasing contractility. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why does squatting soften the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["It slows the heart to a stop","It closes the mitral valve early","More filling reduces obstruction","Less filling increases obstruction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid squatting increases preload, and anything that increases blood flow in the heart reduces the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy by reducing outflow obstruction. Passive leg raising (more preload) and handgrip (more afterload) soften the murmur in the same way. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69 (table and pictures)"},{"q":"Which condition must be differentiated from restrictive cardiomyopathy because the presentations are similar?","choices":["Takotsubo syndrome","Constrictive pericarditis","Aortic dissection","Viral pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis is singled out as the condition that must be differentiated from restrictive cardiomyopathy because the presenting symptoms are so similar. Acute pericarditis, cardiac tamponade, valvular disease and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy round out the differential. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy confirmed?","choices":["Electrocardiogram changes","Catheterization, normal coronaries","Endomyocardial biopsy results","Troponin level above normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed with cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries. Elevated troponin and B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP), ST elevation and mid and apical wall-motion abnormalities all mimic acute coronary syndrome, so normal coronaries are what separate the two. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Adverse ventricular remodeling in dilated cardiomyopathy gives the heart which shape?","choices":["Narrow and tubular","Normal-sized, big atria","Thick septal bulge","Globular, or round"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adverse ventricular remodeling in dilated cardiomyopathy leads to a globular (globe or round) heart. One or both ventricles dilate and contract poorly, total cardiac mass increases with little or no wall hypertrophy, and systolic dysfunction follows. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for acute myocarditis?","choices":["Supportive care","Septal ablation","Early transplant","Long antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care is the mainstay of treatment for acute myocarditis, directed toward the clinical scenario. Some patients need aggressive hemodynamic stabilization, and medications are added as needed: vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, diuretics, antiarrhythmics and anticoagulation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the etiology of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Plaque rupture","Viral infection","Unknown","Amyloid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The etiology of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy is unknown. What is known is the risk factor, a significant emotional or physical stressor such as the death of a loved one, bad financial news or surgery, and that 90% of cases are postmenopausal women. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which laboratory marker indicates cardiac myonecrosis in acute myocarditis?","choices":["Serum amylase","Serum troponin","Serum lipase","Serum ferritin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis in the acute myocarditis work-up. The same panel looks for leukocytosis on the complete blood count and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate that may be elevated, while the electrocardiogram may show sinus tachycardia or arrhythmias. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the gold standard for diagnosing acute myocarditis?","choices":["Serum troponin level","Electrocardiogram","Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard and is used to establish the diagnosis of acute myocarditis once other causes of heart failure have been excluded. It is recommended in acute deterioration of heart function of unknown origin that is not responding to medical treatment. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which storage disorder deposits iron in heart tissue and causes restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Sarcoidosis","Wilson disease","Hemochromatosis","Amyloidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemochromatosis is the storage disorder among the causes of restrictive cardiomyopathy, with iron deposits in heart tissue. The stiffened walls then impair diastolic filling, reduce cardiac output and enlarge both atria. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Where are patients with Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy admitted?","choices":["Home, no admission","To general surgery","To cardiology","To psychiatry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome with no initial reliable differences, so evaluation and treatment follow established acute coronary syndrome protocols and the patient is admitted to cardiology. Care is then empiric and supportive, and most patients completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which drug pair is part of guideline-directed medical therapy for an adult with dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Beta blocker and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","Calcium channel blocker and long-acting nitrate agent","Anticoagulant and antiplatelet agent for every patient","Digoxin with an antiarrhythmic such as amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Guideline-directed medical therapy for adults lists angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, beta blockers, angiotensin receptor blockers and diuretics, and both drugs in this pair are also on the dilated cardiomyopathy drug list. Device therapy and lifestyle changes such as sodium restriction and alcohol cessation sit alongside it. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 42, 105"},{"q":"What is the leading cause of mortality in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Endocarditis","Sudden cardiac death","Aortic dissection","Embolic stroke"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The clinical course of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy varies and heart failure symptoms gradually progress, but sudden cardiac death is the leading cause of mortality. That is why patients are told to keep to mild to moderate noncompetitive activity, stay hydrated and avoid excess alcohol. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Why are both atria enlarged in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Leaking mitral leaflets","Septal outflow blockage","Fluid around the heart","Impaired ventricular filling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, ventricular wall stiffness impairs diastolic filling, so both atria enlarge as they push blood into ventricles that resist filling. Chamber size and systolic function stay reasonably preserved, and atrial fibrillation is common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"When is an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator indicated in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Any reduced ejection fraction at diagnosis","After a first episode of atrial fibrillation","When the pulse pressure becomes narrow","Ejection fraction below 35% on maximal therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) is placed in dilated cardiomyopathy if the ejection fraction remains below 35% after maximum medical therapy. A biventricular ICD combines it with the biventricular pacemaker used for moderately severe left-sided ventricular dysfunction. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Where do prominent Q waves appear on the electrocardiogram in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["No lead shows Q waves","Inferior and lateral leads","Right precordial leads only","Anterior leads V1 to V3 only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, prominent Q waves appear in the inferior leads (II, III and aVF) and the lateral leads (I, aVL and V4 to V6). They reflect septal depolarization of hypertrophied, myopathic tissue and sit alongside left ventricular hypertrophy, atrial enlargement and left axis deviation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve?","choices":["Valve ring stretching as the ventricle dilates","Leaflets thickened by old rheumatic scarring","Anterior leaflet pulled into the outflow tract","Posterior leaflet prolapsing into the atrium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, septal hypertrophy narrows the subaortic outflow tract, and high-velocity turbulent flow pulls the anterior mitral leaflet into the flow tract. That causes mid-systolic obstruction of flow through the left ventricular outflow tract. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the first principle of managing restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Surgical septal myectomy","High-dose diuresis first","Immediate transplantation","Treat the underlying disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management of restrictive cardiomyopathy starts with treating the underlying disorder. Support follows as needed: diuretics with caution for congestion, beta blockers or verapamil and diltiazem to lengthen filling time, anticoagulants for atrial thrombi, a pacemaker for conduction disease and, at the end, transplantation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What produces the third heart sound in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Mitral leaflet striking the septum","Blood striking a compliant ventricle","Atrium contracting on a stiff ventricle","Closure of a thickened aortic valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The third heart sound (S3) gallop of dilated cardiomyopathy is produced by a large amount of blood striking a very compliant left ventricle, a sign of volume overload. It sits with the other cardiac findings: a laterally displaced point of maximal impulse, regurgitant murmurs and jugular vein distention. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which description fits the ventricles in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Stiff, with near-normal cavity size","Ballooned apex that later recovers","Dilated, with impaired contraction","Thick-walled, with a small cavity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is characterized by dilation and impaired contraction of one or both ventricles, which leads to systolic dysfunction. Total cardiac mass rises with little or no wall hypertrophy, the heart becomes globular, and rising end-systolic volumes drive further dilation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does gadolinium add to magnetic resonance imaging in suspected myocarditis?","choices":["Rules out coronary blockage","Measures troponin release","Grows the causative virus","Shows the extent of inflammation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In suspected myocarditis, gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) improves the visibility of inflammation and shows its extent in the myocardium. It sits in the work-up beside troponin, the electrocardiogram, echo and angiography, with endomyocardial biopsy as the gold standard. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the risk factor for Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["A major emotional or physical stressor","Many traditional cardiac risk factors","A flulike illness 1 to 2 weeks before","Inherited sarcomere gene mutation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The risk factor for Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is a significant emotional or physical stressor, such as the death of a loved one, bad financial news, a natural disaster, a new diagnosis, surgery, drug withdrawal or near drowning. About 90% of cases are postmenopausal women, who tend to have few cardiac risk factors. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What happens to ejection fraction in late-stage hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["It rises above 75%","It falls below 25%","It swings every beat","It stays at exactly 60%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy the ejection fraction is usually normal, but in late stages it rises above 75% because the small-volume ventricle nearly empties to maintain cardiac output. Echo also shows septal hypertrophy, a small left ventricular cavity and left atrial enlargement. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which laboratory test evaluates for anemia in suspected dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Complete blood count","Liver function panel","Thyroid function tests","Metabolic panel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complete blood count (CBC) is the test that evaluates for anemia in suspected dilated cardiomyopathy. The rest of the initial panel has its own targets: a comprehensive metabolic panel for electrolytes and renal function, liver function tests and thyroid function. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which inheritance pattern is most common in familial dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Mitochondrial (maternal)","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Familial dilated cardiomyopathy accounts for up to 35% of cases, and autosomal dominant inheritance is the most common pattern. Research has focused on the Lamin A/C genotype, a gene coding proteins critical for the structure and function of the cell nucleus. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"When is anticoagulation given in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Never, because recovery is usual","If a ventricular thrombus is present","For every patient at diagnosis","Only when coronaries are blocked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo treatment is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation: anticoagulation if a left ventricular thrombus is present, diuretics if heart failure is present, and beta blockers are helpful. Patients are admitted to cardiology, and most completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which condition is in the differential diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Athletic heart","Aortic dissection","Takotsubo syndrome","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Athletic heart is on the differential diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, alongside aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation, left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive heart disease and restrictive cardiomyopathy. No specific blood test separates them, and the chest X-ray may be normal. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"When is hospice referral appropriate in cardiomyopathy?","choices":["End-stage cardiomyopathy","At adolescent diagnosis","After a first arrhythmia","Once sodium is restricted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hospice referral is part of palliative care in end-stage cardiomyopathy. Palliative care also covers symptom management for dyspnea, fatigue and edema, advance care planning, goals-of-care discussions and support for caregivers and family. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What is the aim of cardiac rehabilitation programs in cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Reversal of thickened ventricular walls","Replacement of all needed medications","Screening of relatives for gene defects","Better exercise tolerance and quality of life"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation programs aim to improve exercise tolerance and quality of life. Rehabilitative care also includes education on medication adherence, nutrition and symptom monitoring, psychological support for coping with chronic illness, and home-based options for elderly or mobility-limited patients. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"}]};
